of the text will explain why we came to that conclusion.=C2=A0</div><div>Us=
e case</div><div>Flaws in existing solutions</div><div>Proposed solution (t=
his)=C2=A0</div><blockquote class=3D"gmail_quote" style=3D"margin:0px 0px 0=
px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex"><div dir=
=3D"ltr"><div class=3D"gmail_quote"><div></div></div></div></blockquote><di=
v></div><div><br></div><div>&lt;/mglt&gt;=C2=A0</div><blockquote class=3D"g=
mail_quote" style=3D"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204=
,204,204);padding-left:1ex"><div dir=3D"ltr"><div class=3D"gmail_quote"><di=
v>=C2=A0</div><blockquote class=3D"gmail_quote" style=3D"margin:0px 0px 0px=
 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex"><div dir=3D=
"ltr"><div style=3D"font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-siz=
e:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)"><div style=3D"margin:0px;font-size:12pt;font-famil=
y:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif"><div style=3D"margin:0px">
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0However, short-lived certificates ne=
ed to be renewed more frequently<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0than long-lived certificates.=C2=A0 =
If an external CA is unable to issue a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0certificate in time to replace a dep=
loyed certificate, the server<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0would no longer be able to present a=
 valid certificate to clients.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0With short-lived certificates, there=
 is a smaller window of time to<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0renew a certificates and therefore a=
 higher risk that an outage at a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0CA will negatively affect the uptime=
 of the service.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0To reduce the dependency on external=
 CAs, this document proposes a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0limited delegation mechanism that al=
lows a TLS peer to issue its own<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0credentials within the scope of a ce=
rtificate issued by an external<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0CA.=C2=A0 These credentials only ena=
ble the recipient of the delegation to<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0speak for names that the CA has auth=
orized.=C2=A0 For clarity, we will<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0refer to the certificate issued by t=
he CA as a &quot;certificate&quot;, or<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0&quot;delegation certificate&quot;, =
and the one issued by the operator as a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0&quot;delegated credential&quot; or =
&quot;DC&quot;.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">From the text it is unclear why the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">signature scheme appears to be a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">constraint as well how it does not opens<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">to some sort of downgrade attacks if<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">left to the operator. =C2=A0<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;/mglt&gt;<br></div></div></div></div></blockq=
uote><div><br></div><div>The second bullet was left unaddressed, so I added=
 some text here. Downgrades are not a current concern in TLS 1.3 since weak=
 signature algorithms have been removed and clients advertise signature alg=
orithm support and can remove weak algorithms should they arise.=C2=A0</div=
></div></div></blockquote><div>&lt;mglt&gt;</div><div>It is unclear to me i=
f some text has been added to the draft or not, but looking at [1] I do not=
 see additional text, so I interpret the sentence as the text is limited to=
 the email only and is not reflected in the document.=C2=A0</div><div><br><=
/div><div>It is not correct that this is not a current concern with TLS 1.3=
 as the operator could still today use a weaker signature algorithm.=C2=A0 =
In the future, as crypto evolves, it is likely=C2=A0that some signature alg=
orithm will become weak one day. If I understand correctly your text, you e=
nvision that the up-to-date client will remove the weak crypto while the se=
rver will continue to serve legacy clients with weak crypto. If that is the=
 case, this seems to me an issue as the operator does not provides any guar=
antee=C2=A0the security level is equivalent to the one enforced by the CA.=
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=C2=A0</div><div><br></div><div>The text below I=
 believe highlights the problem. names that the CA has authorized is not en=
ough and the delegation should include an agreement of the signature algori=
thm. While the text indicates that the use of alternative signature algorit=
hms may be an advantage, it may also be an inconvenience or potentially a t=
hreat.=C2=A0</div><div><br></div><div>&quot;&quot;&quot;</div><div><span st=
yle=3D"color:rgb(36,41,46);font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,&qu=
ot;Segoe UI&quot;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,=
