_quote" style=3D"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204=
,204);padding-left:1ex"><div dir=3D"ltr"><div style=3D"font-family:Calibri,=
Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)"><div style=3D"m=
argin:0px;font-size:12pt;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif"><d=
iv style=3D"margin:0px">
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">3.2.=C2=A0 Related Work<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Many of the use cases for delegated =
credentials can also be addressed<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0using purely server-side mechanisms =
that do not require changes to<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0client behavior (e.g., a PKCS#11 int=
erface or a remote signing<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0mechanism [KEYLESS]).=C2=A0 These me=
chanisms, however, incur per-<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0transaction latency, since the front=
-end server has to interact with<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0a back-end server that holds a priva=
te key.=C2=A0 The mechanism proposed<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0in this document allows the delegati=
on to be done off-line, with no<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0per-transaction latency.=C2=A0 The f=
igure below compares the message flows<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0for these two mechanisms with TLS 1.=
..3 [RFC8446].<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Remote key signing:<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Client =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =
=C2=A0 =C2=A0Front-End =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0Back-End<br=
>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|----ClientHello---&gt;| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|&lt;---ServerHello----| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|&lt;---Certificate----| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0| =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=
 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 |&lt;---remote sign----&gt;|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|&lt;---CertVerify-----| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0| =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=
.... =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0| =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=
 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Delegated credentials:<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Client =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =
=C2=A0 =C2=A0Front-End =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0Back-End<br=
>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0| =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=
 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 |&lt;--DC distribution-&gt;|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|----ClientHello---&gt;| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|&lt;---ServerHello----| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|&lt;---Certificate----| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|&lt;---CertVerify-----| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0| =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=
.... =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0| =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=
 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0These two mechanisms can be compleme=
ntary.=C2=A0 A server could use<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0credentials for clients that support=
 them, while using [KEYLESS] to<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0support legacy clients.<span>=C2=A0<=
/span><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">I believe that this sentence does not<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">show any complementary as subcert and<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">KEYLESS are targeting different version<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">of TLS, so they can hardly be<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">complementary.=C2=A0 However (and luckily)<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">LURK provides an extension for TLS 1.3<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">[draft-mglt-lurk-tls13] which enable a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">complementary use of these mechanisms<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">these mechanisms. I believe that would<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">be good to indicate the reason they<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">complement each other which is that LURK<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">protects the credentials for its<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">operations. These operations could be<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">performed in the scope of subcert or TLS<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">1.3.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">Note also that in a related section it<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">also worth mentioning that credentials<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">may be managed in different ways and<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">KEYLESS represents an valuable way to<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">protect and manage these credentials in<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">TLS 1.2. However, KEYLESS is known to<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">presents some vulnerabilities (PFS,<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">signing oracle) so the protection<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">remains limited while the LURK extension<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">for TLS 1.2 [draft-mglt-lurk-tls12]<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">addressed these issues and as a result<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">should provide a better protection.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;/mglt&gt;<br></div></div></div></div></blockq=
uote><div><br></div><div>Joe had some comments on this as well. </div></div=
></div></blockquote><div>&lt;mglt&gt; This sentence is cryptic.=C2=A0</div>=
<div>I think - but I might be wrongly interpreting it - your intention is t=
o vaguely insinuate that Joe, as co-chair already responded to that=C2=A0co=
mment and somehow agree that the use of KEYLESS was justified and sufficien=
t. I think the email you are referring to is [1] in which Joe questioned th=
e status of=C2=A0=C2=A0<span style=3D"color:rgb(33,37,41);font-family:SFMon=
o-Regular,Menlo,Monaco,Consolas,&quot;Liberation Mono&quot;,&quot;Courier N=
ew&quot;,monospace;font-size:12.25px;white-space:pre-wrap">draft-mglt-lurk-=
tls13 and Keylessl. This was followed by a response from me and Joe&#39;s r=
